<h2>How should the grouped settings be laid out?</h2>
<p class="subtitle">Same 12 fields, sorted into four groups: Profile, Preferences, Notifications, Security. One Save button submits everything in all three layouts.</p>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card" data-choice="a" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="card-image">
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Stacked sections</div>
        <div class="mockup-body" style="font-size:12px">
          <div class="label">Profile</div>
          <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Display name"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Email"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Avatar URL">
          <hr>
          <div class="label">Preferences</div>
          <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Time zone"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Language"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Theme">
          <hr>
          <div class="label">Notifications</div>
          <div>[ ] Email &nbsp; [ ] Push &nbsp; [ ] Weekly digest</div>
          <hr>
          <div class="label">Security</div>
          <div>[ ] Two-factor auth</div>
          <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Session timeout"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="API token">
          <button class="mock-button">Save</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <h3>A. Stacked sections (recommended)</h3>
      <p>Headed fieldsets on one scrolling page. Plain HTML and CSS, no JS changes, and every field stays visible. 12 fields don't justify hiding any of them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-choice="b" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="card-image">
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Tabs</div>
        <div class="mockup-body" style="font-size:12px">
          <div class="mock-nav"><b>Profile</b> | Preferences | Notifications | Security</div>
          <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Display name"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Email"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Avatar URL">
          <button class="mock-button">Save</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <h3>B. Tabs</h3>
      <p>One group shown at a time. The page is compact, but changes on other tabs are out of sight, and it needs a little JS to switch tabs.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-choice="c" onclick="toggleSelect(this)">
    <div class="card-image">
      <div class="mockup">
        <div class="mockup-header">Sidebar nav + sections</div>
        <div class="mockup-body" style="font-size:12px">
          <div style="display:flex;gap:8px">
            <div class="mock-sidebar" style="min-width:90px">Profile<br>Preferences<br>Notifications<br>Security</div>
            <div class="mock-content">
              <div class="label">Profile</div>
              <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Display name"><input class="mock-input" placeholder="Email">
              <div class="label">Preferences</div>
              <input class="mock-input" placeholder="Time zone">
              <div>...</div>
              <button class="mock-button">Save</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <h3>C. Sidebar anchors + sections</h3>
      <p>A with a jump-link sidebar. Works well on a long page, but it means widening the 640px container, and 12 fields is not long.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
